< The ICH E17 guideline describes regional pooling in multi-regional clinical trials (MRCTs) in terms of the similarity of effect modifier distributions but specifies no similarity measure, and, to our knowledge, no distance between regional effect modifier distributions has been evaluated for this purpose. We quantify how two regions' distributions of each candidate effect modifier differ by the Wasserstein-1 ($W_1$) distance, in original units, with bootstrap confidence intervals. If the conditional average treatment effect is common to all regions, depends on that effect modifier alone, and is Lipschitz with clinical slope $L_{\text{clinical}}$, regional average treatment effects differ by at most $\Delta_{\max} = L_{\text{clinical}} W_1$; when $L_{\text{clinical}}$ is unknown, the smallest slope $L^*$ at which the distance could produce a clinical margin is compared with a sponsor's bound. In simulations, the standardized mean difference stayed at chance in some configurations up to 2,000 patients per region, whereas $W_1$ and the Kolmogorov--Smirnov statistic reached the discrimination target in every configuration; at that target (AUC 0.9), $W_1$ reached it with fewer patients than the Kolmogorov--Smirnov statistic in \numval{nCellsW1FewerKS} of \numval{nCellsSelection} cells, was not separated from it in one, and needed more in a uniform-shift control. For true distances of at least \numval{minW1SdS3toS7} SD in normal-reference scenarios, bootstrap coverage was \numval{covRangeS3toS7N100up2dpAbs} from 100 patients per region. In a hypothetical GUSTO-I-based MRCT, partner rankings on age and systolic blood pressure differed, and a null floor marked which small distances were not evidence of a difference. The framework quantifies differences; the pooling judgment remains clinical.}
