> The ICH E17 guideline describes regional pooling in multi-regional clinical trials (MRCTs) in terms of the similarity of effect modifier distributions but names no measure, and, to our knowledge, no distance between regional effect modifier distributions has been evaluated for this purpose. We quantify how two regions' distributions of each candidate effect modifier differ by the Wasserstein-1 ($W_1$) distance with bootstrap confidence intervals. If the conditional average treatment effect is common to all regions, depends on that modifier alone, and is Lipschitz with clinical slope $L_{\text{clinical}}$, regional average effects differ by at most $\Delta_{\max} = L_{\text{clinical}} W_1$; when $L_{\text{clinical}}$ is unknown, the smallest slope $L^*$ at which the distance could produce a clinical margin is compared with a sponsor's bound. In simulations, the standardized mean difference stayed at chance in some configurations up to 2,000 patients per region, whereas $W_1$ and the Kolmogorov--Smirnov (KS) statistic reached the discrimination target in every configuration; at that target (AUC 0.9), $W_1$ needed fewer patients than the KS statistic in \numval{nCellsW1FewerKS} of \numval{nCellsSelection} cells (three by construction), overlapped it in one, and needed more in a uniform-shift control. For true distances of about \numval{minW1SdS3toS7} to \numval{maxW1SdS3toS7} SD (normal reference, equal $n$), bootstrap coverage was \numval{covRangeS3toS7N100up2dpAbs} from 100 patients per region, below 0.95 beyond Monte Carlo error in \numval{nCellsCovBelow2MCSE} of ten cells. In a hypothetical GUSTO-I-based MRCT, partner rankings on age and on systolic blood pressure differed, and a null floor marked distances not distinguishable from no difference. The framework quantifies differences; the pooling judgment remains clinical.}
