< Despite the regulatory importance of effect modifier distributional similarity, current practice lacks a standardized, clinically calibrated methodology. The ICH E17 guideline provides no specific metric, threshold, or statistical procedure for determining when distributions are ``similar enough.'' Song et al., writing on the NMPA's implementation of ICH E17, identify the pooling strategy as an area where ambiguity remains and describe clustering populations on distances defined from the correlation between effect modifiers and endpoints, citing the reference volume discussed next.\cite{song2025} The pooling-strategy chapter of a reference volume on multi-regional trials under ICH E17 introduces, as one of many possible approaches, cluster analysis of regions on region-level summaries of the candidate effect modifiers: each region is given a representative value for each candidate effect modifier, the values are typically standardized, and distances such as the Euclidean distance between regions are weighted for relevance, for instance through a Lasso regression on the outcome; the chapter notes that the selection of the distance measure is one of the key decisions, and does not evaluate the approach.\cite{komiyama2024} Two gaps remain. First, to our knowledge no distance between regional effect modifier distributions has been proposed and evaluated for this purpose: a single summary per modifier leaves two regions that agree on it but differ in spread, shape, or tail behaviour at distance zero---for a binary characteristic, as in the chapter's illustration, one proportion describes the distribution completely, so the limitation concerns continuous modifiers---and any finite set of summaries requires deciding in advance which distributional features are relevant, rather than comparing the full distributions directly. Second, the chapter calls for a judgment of whether differences in the impact of effect modifiers across pooled regions are ``clinically meaningful or not'' but supplies no metric to operationalize that judgment.
