< \noindent Equation~(\ref{eq:heterogeneity_bound}) is not the only bound of its form. Integration by parts gives $\bar{\tau}_1 - \bar{\tau}_2 = -\int (F_1(x) - F_2(x)) \, d\tau(x)$, so for a CATE of bounded variation on a bounded support, $|\bar{\tau}_1 - \bar{\tau}_2| \leq \mathrm{TV}(\tau) \cdot D_{\text{KS}}$ as well, and $W_1 \leq W_2$ makes $L_{\text{clinical}} \cdot W_2$ a valid but looser bound.\cite{villani2009} Two properties distinguish equation~(\ref{eq:heterogeneity_bound}). The first is attainment. For $\tau^*(x) = L_{\text{clinical}} \int_{x_0}^{x} \operatorname{sgn}(F_2(t) - F_1(t))\,dt$, which is $L_{\text{clinical}}$-Lipschitz, $\bar{\tau}^*_1 - \bar{\tau}^*_2 = L_{\text{clinical}} \int |F_1 - F_2|\,dx = L_{\text{clinical}}\,W_1$, so the bound is the supremum of $|\bar{\tau}_1 - \bar{\tau}_2|$ over all common $L_{\text{clinical}}$-Lipschitz CATEs (the Kantorovich--Rubinstein duality of equation~\ref{eq:dual}): every valid bound with the same constant, including $L_{\text{clinical}} \cdot W_2$ and, on a support $[a, b]$, $L_{\text{clinical}} (b - a) D_{\text{KS}}$, is at least as large, and the $L^*$ of Section~\ref{sec:inference} is exactly the smallest slope at which some such CATE produces the margin. On a risk-difference scale $|\tau| \leq 1$, so $\tau^*$ is admissible when $L_{\text{clinical}} (b - a) \leq 2$; for the bounds and supports of Section~\ref{sec:application} the products are \numval{lipRangeAge} (age) and \numval{lipRangeSbp} (SBP). The second is the constant: $L_{\text{clinical}}$ is a change in treatment effect per unit of the effect modifier, the kind of quantity that treatment-by-covariate interaction analyses estimate, whereas $\mathrm{TV}(\tau)$ is the total excursion of the CATE over the support, which reduces to a clinically stated quantity only for a monotone CATE. Where both constants are available, neither bound dominates in general. On a support $[a, b]$, $W_1 \leq (b - a)\,D_{\text{KS}}$ and $\mathrm{TV}(\tau) \leq L_{\text{clinical}}\,(b - a)$, and $L_{\text{clinical}} \cdot W_1 \leq \mathrm{TV}(\tau) \cdot D_{\text{KS}}$ holds exactly when $W_1 / D_{\text{KS}} \leq \mathrm{TV}(\tau) / L_{\text{clinical}}$. For a CATE whose slope has constant magnitude $L_{\text{clinical}}$, such as a linear CATE, $\mathrm{TV}(\tau) = L_{\text{clinical}}(b - a)$ and equation~(\ref{eq:heterogeneity_bound}) is never the looser of the two; the Kolmogorov--Smirnov bound gains as the CATE's slope falls below its maximum over much of the support, most sharply when the CATE changes steeply over a narrow range---the threshold case in which equation~(\ref{eq:heterogeneity_bound}) is itself conservative (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).
