< where $\hat{F}_r$ denotes the empirical CDF (right-continuous) of region $r$ and $x_{(1)} < \cdots < x_{(m)}$ are the $m$ distinct values of the pooled sample (ties are pervasive when an effect modifier is recorded on a coarse grid, as with systolic blood pressure recorded in whole mmHg in Section~\ref{sec:application}). The estimator $\widehat{W}_1$ inherits the original measurement units of the effect modifier. The asymptotic distribution of $W_1$ is non-standard: del Barrio et al.\cite{delbarrio1999} showed that $\sqrt{n}\,W_1(\hat{F}_n, F) \xrightarrow{d} \int |B(F(x))|\,dx$, where $B$ is a standard Brownian bridge. Because the quantiles of this limiting distribution depend on the unknown $F$, no universal critical values are available. In the two-sample case a Gaussian limit, and hence a Wald interval, exists under the measure-zero condition stated below, but the condition cannot be verified from data and the limit's variance involves $\operatorname{sgn}(F_1 - F_2)$, which is unstable where the CDFs cross. We therefore employ the nonparametric percentile bootstrap with $B = 2{,}000$ replicates.\cite{efron1993} Each bootstrap replicate draws $n_1$ observations with replacement from region 1 and, independently, $n_2$ from region 2, and recomputes $\widehat{W}_1$ by equation~(\ref{eq:estimator}); the 95\% interval is formed by the 2.5th and 97.5th sample percentiles of the $B$ replicates (R's default quantile definition, type 7). In the two-sample setting, when the set $\{x : F_1(x) = F_2(x) \in (0,1)\}$ has Lebesgue measure zero---for example, when the two CDFs cross only at isolated points---the Hadamard derivative of the $L_1$ functional at $F_1 - F_2$ is linear, and the ordinary nonparametric bootstrap is consistent by the functional delta method.\cite{fang2019} Where the CDFs coincide on an interval, including $F_1 = F_2$, the derivative is non-linear and the naive bootstrap is not consistent.\cite{dumbgen1993,fang2019,sommerfeld2018} Convergence occurs at rate $\sqrt{n_1 n_2/(n_1+n_2)}$ (see Appendix~\ref{app:asymptotic} for details). The estimator~(\ref{eq:estimator}) is computed in $O((n_1 + n_2) \log(n_1 + n_2))$ time, dominated by sorting the combined order statistics.
