< In one dimension, the empirical $W_1$ distance converges at rate $O(n^{-1/2})$ if and only if $\int \sqrt{F(x)(1-F(x))}\,dx < \infty$.\cite{delbarrio1999} For two samples with $n_1/(n_1+n_2) \to \lambda \in (0,1)$, when $\int \sqrt{F_r(1-F_r)}\,dx < \infty$ ($r = 1, 2$) and $\{x : F_1(x) = F_2(x) \in (0,1)\}$ has Lebesgue measure zero, the directional derivative of $W_1$ at $(F_1, F_2)$ is linear, which is the condition under which the bootstrap statement below holds;\cite{fang2019} this condition excludes $F_1 = F_2$. At the boundary $F_1 = F_2$ the derivative is non-linear and the limit is not Gaussian; separately, a percentile interval cannot contain zero, which is the degenerate coverage observed under the null (S1; Section~\ref{sec:sim_results}). For an effect modifier recorded on a grid, the CDFs are step functions and the condition fails whenever they agree at any grid point inside the support; the finite-space results of Sommerfeld and Munk then apply.\cite{sommerfeld2018} Their limit is Gaussian only when the dual solution is unique, which fails if the two sample CDFs coincide on some interval between consecutive grid values; for the systolic blood pressure comparisons of Section~\ref{sec:application}, the sample CDFs of Region~8 and the partner coincide on such an interval in \numval{sbpCdfCoincidePairs} of the 15 pairs.
